<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabih az-Zubayr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1842–<a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabih az-Zubayr was a Sudanese warlord and slave trader who built a powerful state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region in the late nineteenth century. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> in 1893 and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>. He was killed by French forces in 1900, but his advance was the first major external threat to reach the Wadai Empire's heartland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr.jpg" alt="Rabih az-Zubayr" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arrantzaleak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rabih_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rabih was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfaya">Halfaya al-Muluk</a>, later a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>; he was of Nilotic, possibly Shanqella, origin and was enslaved as a child. At about twenty he joined the Egyptian army, serving mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, then went to the Bahr el Ghazal for the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves and entered the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zubayr-rahma-mansur">Al-Zubayr Rahma Mansur</a>. Under Mansur's son Suleiman he became a leading commander. When Suleiman's rebellion of 1878 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> failed and the surrendered officers were executed, Rabih escaped westwards with about 400 soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1879 the officers' council, the Zubat al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubar">Kubar</a>, elected him emir. Over the next twelve years he raided in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a>, built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> called zeribas and grew his force by offering prisoners their lives and freedom if they joined him. By the early 1890s he had about 5,000 men and 44 light field guns. In 1887 he accepted the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a>, and he defeated the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagirmi">Bagirmi</a>.</p><p>In the summer of 1893 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> proper with about 3,000 men, about half of them armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> or rifles; Shehu Ashimi sent against him an army under Momman Tahr that may have numbered 30,000. Once Bornu was conquered, for the first time in over a thousand years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem–Bornu Empire</a> was under foreign rule. Rabih ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikwa">Dikwa</a> as a military dictator with the Zubat al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubar">Kubar</a>, keeping submissive local rulers under his commanders' supervision. He levied a fixed annual tax of one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> thaler on every person, collected brutally; his army, perhaps 35,000 strong, was among the best equipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised him as sultan of Borno, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared him illegitimate and presented his state as a slave economy. In July 1899 his forces destroyed a French advance guard at the Battle of Togbao, killing Henri Bretonnet. After his death at Kousséri his head was displayed on a pike, and a photograph of it appeared in L'Illustration in March 1901. His son Fadlallah fought on until he was killed at Gujba on 23 August 1901, and the shehus were restored under colonial rule. Rabih is remembered both for his brutal rule and for his resistance to European colonialism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rabih az-Zubayr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1893 the Sudanese warlord <b>Rabih az-Zubayr</b> conquered Bornu and destroyed its capital at Kukawa, which ended the independence of Kanem–Bornu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of <b>Rabih az-Zubayr</b>, a Sudanese warlord who ravaged Bagirmi and conquered Bornu in 1893, brought a strong new military power to Wadai's borders before the French arrived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the French killed <b>Rabih</b> in 1900, the European powers restored the al-Kanemi dynasty, but the old lands of the Kanem-Bornu Empire were split by the borders of Nigeria, Kamerun and Chad.</blockquote><a href="/state/dikwa-emirate">Dikwa Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rabih az-Zubayr</b> had served the slave-trading magnate al-Zubayr Rahma Mansur in the Bahr al-Ghazal.</blockquote><a href="/state/rabih-az-zubayrs-state">Rabih az-Zubayr's state</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kousseri">Kousséri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.6,32.53&amp;year=1879" aria-label="Khartoum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rabih az-Zubayr"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 6216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 6216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.6,32.53&amp;year=1879">What was here in 1879? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
